\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For integers $m\ge2$ and $k\ge1$, the Erd\H{o}s--Moser equation is
\[
\sum_{j=1}^{m-1}j^k=m^k.
\]
The pair $(m,k)=(3,1)$ gives $1+2=3$.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is $(m,k)=(3,1)$ its only solution with $m\ge2$ and $k\ge1$? Equivalently, does it have no solution with $k\ge2$? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1011.2940}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is one plus two equals three the only positive-integer power-sum equality of this form?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 9400105 (AMR-093-0105). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Pieter Moree, \emph{Moser\textquotesingle s mathemagical work on the equation $1^k+\cdots+(m-1)^k=m^k$}, arXiv:1011.2940v2 (2011) Introduction, equation (1), p. 1. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1011.2940}.
\item[S3] Wikipedia contributors, \emph{List of unsolved problems in mathematics}, revision 1366636767 (2026) Number theory, Erdős–Moser problem. \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=List_of_unsolved_problems_in_mathematics&oldid=1366636767#Number_theory}.
